{"id": "z1761", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 11, "onset_chunk": 10, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So Paul had 7 1 books initially. After selling some, he bought 38 new ones and ended up with 116. Let's figure out how many he sold first. If we add the 3 8 he bought to his final count of 1 16, that gives us 1 5 4 books in total after buying them. Since he started with 71, he must have sold 1 54 minus 71, which is 83 books. Now, to find out how many more he bought than sold, we subtract the number sold from the number bought: 3 8 minus 83 equal s negative 45. Wait, that doesn't make sense because you can't buy less than you sell if you end up with more books. Let' s try a different approach. We know he has 116 now and bought 3 8, so before buying, he had 1 16 minus 38, which is 7 8 books. But he started with 71, so he actually gained 7 books through selling and buying? No, let' s simplify this. He had 71, then sold some to get to a point where adding 38 got him to 1 16. So,", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
